<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="cross-product-complete">
    <title>Cross Product (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        The dot product gave us a (geometrically meaningful) way to multiply to vectors to get a scalar. For 
                        3D vectors, we can also multiply vectors to get another (geometrically meaningful) vector.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="6in">
            <title>Cross Product</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                If <m>\vvec = \langle x_1, y_1,z_1 \rangle</m> and <m>\wvec = \langle x_2, y_2,z_2 \rangle </m>, then 
                <md>
                    \vvec \times \wvec = 
                </md>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        <m>\langle y_1z_2 - y_2z_1, x_2z_1 - x_1z_2, x_1y_2 - x_2y_1 \rangle</m>.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        This computation comes from a <term>determinant</term> (more later), and there is a trick for 
                        remembering the pattern (see class notes).
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Note that <m>\vvec \times \wvec</m> has both magnitude and direction. The magnitude gives us a measure
                        of how close to parallel/perpendicular the two vectors are.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        As long as <m>\vvec \times \wvec</m> is not the zero vector (ie., the two vectors are not parallel),
                        the vector <m>\vvec \times \wvec</m> points in a direction which is perpendicular to both <m>\vvec</m> 
                        and <m>\wvec</m>. There are two possibilities here; to determine the correct one, use 
                        the <term>right-hand rule</term>.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Note that <m>\vvec \times \wvec = - (\wvec \times \vvec)</m> (so this product does not commute!).
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
            <exercise workspace="3in">
                <title>Example: Cross Product </title>
             
                <statement>
                    <p>
                       Compute <m>\mathbf{i} \times \mathbf{j}</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>\mathbf{i} \times \mathbf{j} = \langle 0,0,1 \rangle = \mathbf{k}</m>. This is why we must take care when drawing
                    a 3D coordinate system.
                </p>
            </solution>
          
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
        <exercise workspace="8in">
        <title>Geometric Applications</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
            Given vectors <m>\uvec</m>, <m>\vvec</m>, <m>\wvec</m> in 3-dimensional space, the volume of the 
        parallelepiped formed is:
            </p>
           
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <m>|\uvec \cdot (\vvec \times \wvec)|</m>. In the future, we will see that the concept of <term>linear independence</term> between vectors
                is an important one. In 3D, three vectors are linearly independent if they do not lie in the same plane.
            </p>
            <p>
                We can use the geometric interpretation above to determine if a set of 3 vectors is linearly independent (non-zero volume)
                or linearly dependent (zero volume).
            </p>
        </solution>
            
        </exercise>
</page>
<page>

        <exercise workspace="5in">
        <title>Examples: Linear Independence</title>
       <statement>
        <p>
            Show that <m>\vvec = \langle 1,4,-7  \rangle</m>, <m>\wvec = \langle 2,-1,4  \rangle</m>, and <m>\uvec = \langle 0,-9,18  \rangle</m>
            are linearly dependent vectors.
        </p>
       </statement> 
       <solution>
        <p>
            We can compute that <m>\vvec \cdot (\wvec \times \uvec) = 0</m>, which means that these vectors lie in the same plane, and 
            are linearly dependent.
        </p>
       </solution>
        </exercise>
    
    </page>


       
    </worksheet>
